 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** Expected number of steps is proportional to the ratio of the majorant to the local extinction coefficient. In homogeneous regions, it converges quickly. In sparse regions with a loose global majorant, it can be $O(\bar{\sigma}_t / \sigma_t)$, which is inefficient [S9, S11, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per ray, excluding the volume data structure storage. It does not require storing intermediate samples along the ray [S1, book][S12].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a 51 million particle HACC dataset, delta tracking path tracing achieved 1.44 MR/s, compared to 6.0 MR/s for ray casting, but with higher quality and unbiased results [S2, book]. Rendering a cloud with delta tracking and Next Event Estimation (10 samples/pixel) took 248s [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Unbiased; handles arbitrary heterogeneity without grid constraints; simple implementation; no fixed step size bias [S6][S10, unofficial][S12].
-*   **Limitations:** High variance in sparse regions with loose majorants; requires efficient volume queries; many null collisions waste computation [S9, S11, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Unbiased path tracing in heterogeneous media; particle volumes; when grid-based methods are impractical [S2, book][S12].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time rendering with strict latency constraints in sparse volumes (use ray marching or ratio tracking with optimizations); homogeneous media (use direct exponential sampling) [S9, unofficial][S12].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Expected number of steps is proportional to the ratio of the majorant to the average extinction coefficient. In sparse volumes with a loose majorant, this can be very high [S11, S13, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal additional memory beyond the volume data structure and the majorant. No need for structured grids or precomputed transmittance tables [S1, book][S14].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the HACC dataset (51 million particles), delta tracking path tracing achieved 1.44 MR/s, compared to 6.0 MR/s for ray casting, but with higher quality and unbiasedness [S2, book]. Rendering a cloud with delta tracking and Next Event Estimation took 248s for 10 samples per pixel [S11, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Unbiased, handles arbitrary heterogeneous media (including particle volumes and unstructured grids), no need for voxelization [S2, book][S14].
+*   **Limitations:** High variance in sparse volumes with loose majorants; many null collisions waste computation [S11, S13, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When unbiased rendering of complex, unstructured, or highly heterogeneous media is required, and performance constraints allow for higher sample counts [S2, book][S14].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For homogeneous media (use analytical transmittance) or when real-time performance is critical and bias is acceptable (use ray marching) [S14][S17, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
 
-Delta tracking, also known as the null-collision method or Woodcock tracking, was developed independently in the 1950s and 1960s for neutron transport and plasma physics [S6][S14, unofficial]. Key contributors include Butcher and Messel (1958, 1960), Zerby et al. (1961), Bertini (1963), Woodcock et al. (1965), Miller (1967), and Coleman (1968) [S14, unofficial]. It was adopted in computer graphics for unbiased volume rendering to avoid the bias of ray marching [S6][S10, unofficial]. It is implemented in systems like OSPRay using ISPC and Open VKL for RBF particle volumes [S2, book].
+Delta tracking, also known as Woodcock tracking, was initially developed in the 1950s and 1960s by Butcher and Messel (1958, 1960), Zerby et al. (1961), Bertini (1963), Woodcock et al. (1965), Miller (1967), and Coleman (1968) [S16, unofficial]. It was originally designed for neutron transport simulations in heterogeneous media [S16, unofficial]. In computer graphics, it has been adopted for unbiased volumetric path tracing to handle complex participating media without the bias of ray marching [S14][S12, unofficial]. It is implemented in systems like OSPRay using ISPC and exposed via the Open VKL API for volume rendering of RBF particle data [S2, book].
 
